// Generated by scripts/build-glossary-data.mjs from content-source/glossary. Do not edit by hand.
export const GLOSSARY_SHARD = Object.freeze({"bitcoin-halving":{"slug":"bitcoin-halving","cat":"basics","en":"Bitcoin halving","contentLocale":"en","term":"Bitcoin halving","aka":["halving","halvening"],"short":"A Bitcoin halving is a pre-programmed event, roughly every four years, that cuts the number of new bitcoins paid to miners for each block by 50 percent.","body":[[["Bitcoin","bitcoin"],"'s rules release new coins through the block reward. The halving happens every 210,000 blocks, which at about 10 minutes per block works out to roughly four years. The reward started at 50 BTC in 2009, dropped to 25, 12.5, 6.25 and, after the April 2024 halving, 3.125 BTC per block."],"This schedule is why Bitcoin's supply approaches 21 million but never exceeds it. Each halving slows the growth of new supply, and the last fractions of a bitcoin are expected to be mined around the year 2140. After that, miners will rely on transaction fees alone.","For miners, a halving instantly cuts revenue from new coins in half, so less efficient operations may shut down unless the price or fees make up the difference. For traders, halvings attract a lot of attention and narratives, but past price moves around halvings came with many other factors and do not guarantee any future result."],"example":["Before the April 2024 halving, the reward was 6.25 BTC per block. With about 144 blocks a day, miners earned roughly 900 new BTC daily. After the halving to 3.125 BTC, that fell to about 450 BTC per day. At an illustrative price of 60,000 ",["USDT","usdt"],", daily new-coin revenue for all miners combined dropped from about 54 million to 27 million USDT, not counting fees."],"mistakes":["Treating the halving as a guaranteed price catalyst instead of a supply schedule change.","Thinking the halving cuts existing holdings in half; it only affects newly issued coins.","Expecting the halving on an exact calendar date, when it is set by block height and can shift by days or weeks."],"faq":[{"q":"When is the next Bitcoin halving?","a":"It is expected around 2028, after block 1,050,000 is mined. The exact date depends on how fast blocks are produced."},{"q":"What happens when all bitcoins are mined?","a":"Miners will no longer receive new BTC and will be paid only through transaction fees."},{"q":"Does the halving affect my BTC balance?","a":"No. Your coins stay exactly the same. Only the reward for new blocks changes."}],"title":"What is the Bitcoin halving? Meaning and schedule","description":"Learn how the Bitcoin halving works, how the block reward fell to 3.125 BTC and what it means for miners and supply.","related":[["bitcoin","Bitcoin (BTC)","Bitcoin (BTC) is the first cryptocurrency, a decentralized digital money with a hard cap of 21 million coins that runs on its own proof-of-work blockchain."],["mining","Crypto mining","Crypto mining is the process of using specialised computers to secure a proof-of-work blockchain by adding new blocks, in exchange for newly issued coins and transaction fees."],["proof-of-work","Proof of work (PoW)","Proof of work (PoW) is a consensus mechanism in which miners use computing power to solve a puzzle, and the first to find a valid answer earns the right to add the next block and collect the reward."],["hash-rate","Hash rate","Hash rate is the total computing power that miners use to secure a proof-of-work blockchain, measured in hashes per second."],["satoshi","Satoshi (sats)","A satoshi (sat) is the smallest unit of Bitcoin, equal to 0.00000001 BTC, so one bitcoin contains 100,000,000 satoshis."]],"lesson":null,"coin":{"slug":"bitcoin","name":"Bitcoin","symbol":"BTC"},"tool":null,"cta":false},"stop-loss":{"slug":"stop-loss","cat":"trading","en":"Stop-loss order","contentLocale":"en","term":"Stop-loss order","aka":["stop loss","SL","stop"],"short":"A stop-loss order is an order that automatically closes your position when the price reaches a level you set, limiting how much you can lose on a trade.","body":[["You choose a trigger price below your entry for a long position, or above it for a short. When the market reaches that trigger, the exchange sends a ",["market order","market-order"]," or a ",["limit order","limit-order"]," to close the position. Exchanges may let you choose whether the trigger uses the ",["last traded price","last-price"]," or the ",["mark price","mark-price"],"."],["A stop-loss turns an open-ended risk into a planned one. If you know where your stop is, you can size the position so that being stopped out costs a fixed share of your account, such as 1 or 2 percent. On leveraged positions, a stop placed well before your ",["liquidation price","liquidation-price"]," protects you from losing the whole margin."],["Stops are not perfect. In a fast crash or a price gap, a market stop can fill noticeably below the trigger because of ",["slippage","slippage"],", and a stop-limit may not fill at all. Placing a stop too close to the entry can get you stopped out by normal volatility, so set it where your trade idea is proven wrong."]],"example":"Suppose you have a 10,000 USDT account and you want to risk at most 1 percent, or 100 USDT. You plan to buy a coin at 50 USDT with a stop-loss at 46, a risk of 4 USDT per coin. That means you can buy 25 coins, a position of 1,250 USDT. If the price falls to 46, the stop sells and you lose about 100 USDT plus fees and any slippage, instead of watching the loss grow.","mistakes":["Moving the stop-loss further away when the price approaches it.","Setting stops at obvious round numbers where many other stops cluster.","Relying on a stop placed beyond the liquidation price on a leveraged position."],"faq":[{"q":"Where should I put my stop-loss?","a":"Place it at a price that would show your trade idea is wrong, such as below a key support level, then size the position so the loss at that level fits your risk limit."},{"q":"Can a stop-loss fail?","a":"A stop-market order will almost always execute, but it can fill at a worse price during gaps or crashes. A stop-limit can fail to fill if the price moves past your limit."},{"q":"Does a stop-loss protect me from liquidation?","a":"It can, if it triggers before the liquidation price. Check both levels on your futures position."}],"title":"What is a stop-loss order in crypto? Meaning","description":"Learn how a stop-loss order works, how to size a trade around it and why stops can still fill at a worse price.","related":[["take-profit","Take-profit order","A take-profit order is an order that automatically closes all or part of your position when the price reaches a target level, locking in gains without you having to watch the market."],["stop-limit-order","Stop-limit order","A stop-limit order combines a stop price and a limit price: when the market reaches the stop price, a limit order is placed at your limit price, so it only fills at that price or better."],["trailing-stop","Trailing stop","A trailing stop is a stop order that automatically follows the price at a set distance as it moves in your favour, then triggers if the price reverses by that distance."],["position-sizing","Position sizing","Position sizing is deciding how much to put into a single trade so that, if your stop-loss is hit, you lose only a planned share of your account."],["liquidation","Liquidation","Liquidation is when an exchange forcibly closes your leveraged position because your margin has fallen below the maintenance margin requirement."]],"lesson":{"id":"E17","slug":"stop-loss-is-a-plan","ep":17,"title":"Take-profit and stop-loss are plans, not insurance"},"coin":null,"tool":"/liquidations","cta":false},"unrealized-pnl":{"slug":"unrealized-pnl","cat":"derivatives","en":"Unrealized PnL","contentLocale":"en","term":"Unrealized PnL","aka":["unrealized profit and loss","floating PnL","paper profit","uPnL"],"short":"Unrealized PnL is the profit or loss on a position that is still open, calculated from your entry price and the current price, and it only becomes real when you close the position.","body":["PnL stands for profit and loss. While a position is open, its value changes with every price move, and the exchange shows this as unrealized PnL. Once you close the position, the result is locked in as realized PnL and added to or taken from your balance.",["On futures exchanges, unrealized PnL is usually calculated with the ",["mark price","mark-price"],", not the ",["last traded price","last-price"],". Exchanges often also show ROE or ROI, which is unrealized PnL divided by your margin. With ",["leverage","leverage"],", ROE can look dramatic: a 2% price move at 25x shows as a 50% gain or loss on margin."],["Unrealized PnL matters for risk. A growing unrealized loss reduces your margin balance and brings you closer to ",["liquidation","liquidation"],". In ",["cross margin mode","cross-margin"],", unrealized profit on one position can support others, but it can disappear quickly if the market turns. Some exchanges limit how much unrealized profit you can withdraw or use as margin."]],"example":["Suppose you open a long of 3 SOL perpetual contracts, each worth 1 SOL, at 150 ",["USDT","usdt"],". The mark price rises to 162. Your unrealized PnL is 3 × 12 = 36 USDT. If you used 90 USDT of margin (5x), the exchange shows an ROE of 36 ÷ 90 = 40%. If the price falls back to 150 before you close, that 36 USDT disappears and your realized PnL is zero minus fees."],"mistakes":["Counting unrealized profit as money already earned.","Judging a trade by ROE percentage without looking at the actual amount at risk.","Forgetting that fees and funding reduce the realized result compared with the displayed unrealized PnL."],"faq":[{"q":"What is the difference between realized and unrealized PnL?","a":"Unrealized PnL is the current paper result of an open position. Realized PnL is the final result after you close it, including fees."},{"q":"Why is my unrealized PnL different from what the chart suggests?","a":"Exchanges usually calculate it with the mark price, while the chart shows the last traded price. Fees may also be estimated in."}],"title":"What is unrealized PnL? Meaning in crypto trading","description":"Learn what unrealized PnL means, how it is calculated with mark price and how it differs from realized profit and ROE.","related":[["mark-price","Mark price","The mark price is a fair-value price an exchange uses to calculate unrealized profit and loss and to trigger liquidations, instead of the last traded price."],["cross-margin","Cross margin","Cross margin is a margin mode where all your open positions share the available balance in your account as collateral, so profits and spare funds can absorb losses before liquidation."],["liquidation-price","Liquidation price","The liquidation price is the mark price at which your leveraged position will be forcibly closed because your margin would fall to the maintenance margin level."],["take-profit","Take-profit order","A take-profit order is an order that automatically closes all or part of your position when the price reaches a target level, locking in gains without you having to watch the market."]],"lesson":{"id":"E20","slug":"maintenance-margin","ep":20,"title":"Why you can be closed out before your margin hits zero"},"coin":null,"tool":null,"cta":false},"cold-wallet":{"slug":"cold-wallet","cat":"wallets-security","en":"Cold wallet (cold storage)","contentLocale":"en","term":"Cold wallet (cold storage)","aka":["cold storage","offline wallet"],"short":"A cold wallet is a way of storing crypto where the private keys are generated and kept offline, away from internet-connected devices, so remote attackers cannot reach them.","body":[["Cold storage covers hardware wallets, air-gapped computers or phones that never go online, and paper or metal backups of keys. The coins still sit on the ",["blockchain","blockchain"],"; what is cold is the key that signs transactions."],"To spend from cold storage you create a transaction, sign it on the offline device, and then broadcast it from an online device. This extra step is the point: even if your laptop is infected, the key never touches it.","Exchanges use cold storage too, often with multisig and several people needed to approve a movement. For individuals, cold storage is best for long-term holdings. The main risks shift to physical ones: loss, fire, theft of the backup, or forgetting where it is."],"example":["Imagine you hold 1.5 ",["ETH","ethereum"]," that you do not plan to touch for two years. You keep 0.1 ETH in a phone wallet for gas and small swaps and send 1.4 ETH to a ",["hardware wallet","hardware-wallet"],", with the 24-word backup stamped on a steel plate in a separate place from the device. If your phone is hacked, the attacker can reach 0.1 ETH but not the 1.4 ETH, because signing those coins requires the physical device and its PIN."],"mistakes":["Calling a wallet cold while its seed phrase is stored as a photo on an online phone.","Keeping the device and its only backup in the same drawer.","Never testing a restore from the backup before relying on it."],"faq":[{"q":"What is the difference between a hot and a cold wallet?","a":"A hot wallet keeps keys on an internet-connected device, while a cold wallet keeps them offline. Hot is convenient for daily use, cold is better for savings."},{"q":"Can a cold wallet be hacked?","a":"Remote attacks are much harder, but you can still lose funds by signing a malicious transaction, revealing the seed phrase, or buying a tampered device from an unofficial seller."}],"title":"What is a cold wallet? Cold storage meaning","description":"Learn what a cold wallet is, how offline signing protects your keys, and which risks remain with cold storage.","related":[["hot-wallet","Hot wallet","A hot wallet is a crypto wallet whose private keys are stored on a device connected to the internet, such as a phone app, browser extension or exchange system, which makes it convenient but more exposed to attacks."],["hardware-wallet","Hardware wallet","A hardware wallet is a small physical device that stores your private keys offline and signs transactions inside the device, so the keys never leave it even when it is plugged into a computer or phone."],["seed-phrase","Seed phrase (recovery phrase)","A seed phrase is a list of usually 12 or 24 ordinary words that encodes the master key of a crypto wallet, so anyone who has the words can restore the wallet and spend all of its funds."],["multisig-wallet","Multisig wallet","A multisig wallet is a crypto wallet that needs signatures from several separate keys, such as 2 of 3, before any transaction can be sent, so no single lost or stolen key can move the funds."]],"lesson":{"id":"E03","slug":"exchange-vs-wallet","ep":3,"title":"Where are my coins? Exchange vs wallet"},"coin":null,"tool":null,"cta":false},"rug-pull":{"slug":"rug-pull","cat":"wallets-security","en":"Rug pull","contentLocale":"en","term":"Rug pull","aka":["rugpull","rugged","exit scam"],"short":"A rug pull is a scam where the creators of a crypto project suddenly drain its liquidity, dump their tokens or abandon it after attracting buyers, leaving the token close to worthless.","body":[["Rug pulls are most common with new tokens on decentralized exchanges, especially memecoins. A typical pattern: the team creates a token, adds a ",["liquidity pool","liquidity-pool"],", promotes it hard, and once buyers have added value, removes the ",["liquidity","liquidity"]," or sells a large hidden allocation. The price collapses in minutes."],["Some rugs are coded in from the start, for example a ",["contract","smart-contract"]," that blocks holders from selling, lets the owner mint unlimited tokens, or charges a sell tax the owner can raise to 100 percent. Others are slow: the team stops building and sells gradually."],"Things to check before buying a new token: whether liquidity is locked and for how long, who controls the contract and whether ownership is renounced, how much supply sits in a few wallets, and whether you can actually sell a small amount."],"example":["Imagine a new token launches with a pool holding 50 ",["ETH","ethereum"]," and 1 billion tokens. Over two days buyers add another 150 ETH, pushing the pool to 200 ETH. The deployer, who kept the LP tokens unlocked, withdraws all the liquidity and receives about 200 ETH plus the remaining tokens. Holders who bought the token now find almost no ETH left in the pool, so their tokens can only be sold for a tiny fraction of what they paid."],"mistakes":["Buying a token because it is trending without checking who controls the liquidity.","Assuming a token is safe because it is listed on a DEX, where anyone can create a pool.","Not testing a small sell before buying more, which would reveal a honeypot contract."],"faq":[{"q":"How can I tell if a token might be a rug pull?","a":"Look for unlocked liquidity, an owner who can mint or change taxes, a few wallets holding most of the supply, and anonymous teams with no track record. None of these checks is a guarantee."},{"q":"Is a rug pull illegal?","a":"Deliberately deceiving buyers can be fraud in many countries, but enforcement depends on the jurisdiction and whether the team can be identified."}],"title":"What is a rug pull in crypto? How to spot one","description":"What a crypto rug pull is, how liquidity drains and malicious contracts work, and the checks to make before buying a new token.","related":[["memecoin","Memecoin","A memecoin is a cryptocurrency inspired by an internet joke, animal or cultural trend, whose price is driven mainly by community attention rather than a product or cash flow."],["liquidity-pool","Liquidity pool","A liquidity pool is a smart contract holding two or more tokens that traders swap against on a DEX, funded by liquidity providers who earn a share of the trading fees."],["token-approval","Token approval (allowance)","A token approval is a permission you sign in your wallet that lets a smart contract spend a set amount of a specific token from your address, often needed before swapping or depositing in DeFi."],["dex","Decentralized exchange (DEX)","A decentralized exchange (DEX) is a trading platform that runs on smart contracts, letting you swap tokens directly from your own wallet without depositing funds with a company."]],"lesson":{"id":"E11","slug":"scam-red-flags","ep":11,"title":"Someone says he can make you money. First, see what he wants."},"coin":null,"tool":"/topics/meme-coins","cta":false},"oracle":{"slug":"oracle","cat":"defi","en":"Blockchain oracle","contentLocale":"en","term":"Blockchain oracle","aka":["oracle","price oracle","price feed"],"short":"A blockchain oracle is a service that brings outside data, most often asset prices, onto a blockchain so smart contracts can use it for actions like liquidations, settlements and swaps.","body":[["Smart contracts can only read data already on their own chain. To know the price of ",["ETH","ethereum"]," in dollars, a lending protocol needs someone to publish that price on-chain. Oracle networks such as Chainlink and Pyth collect prices from many sources and publish them, often with several independent ",["node","node"]," operators."],"Oracles decide when loans get liquidated, how perp DEXs mark positions, and how synthetic assets are priced. If an oracle reports a wrong price, even briefly, contracts act on it.",["Many ",["DeFi","defi"]," exploits came from weak oracle design, for example using the spot price of a single thin ",["DEX","dex"]," pool, which an attacker can move with a large trade or a ",["flash loan","flash-loan"],". Stronger setups use aggregated prices, time-weighted averages and sanity checks."]],"example":"Suppose a lending protocol reads the price of a small token from one DEX pool holding only 200,000 dollars. An attacker uses a flash loan to buy the token in that pool, pushing its price from 1 to 4 dollars. The protocol now values the attacker's 100,000 tokens of collateral at 400,000 dollars instead of 100,000, and the attacker borrows against that inflated value. An aggregated oracle using many markets would have shown a far smaller move.","mistakes":["Assuming a DeFi price is always the same as the price on major exchanges.","Ignoring which oracle a lending or perp protocol uses before depositing.","Thinking oracles are only about prices, when they can also deliver data such as random numbers or event outcomes."],"faq":[{"q":"What is the oracle problem?","a":"Blockchains cannot verify outside data by themselves, so contracts must trust the oracle's data. The challenge is making that data hard to manipulate or fake."},{"q":"Is Chainlink an oracle?","a":"Yes. Chainlink is one of the most widely used decentralized oracle networks, providing price feeds and other data to many blockchains."}],"title":"What is a blockchain oracle? Price feeds in DeFi","description":"What blockchain oracles are, why DeFi relies on price feeds, and how oracle manipulation has caused exploits.","related":[["smart-contract","Smart contract","A smart contract is a program stored on a blockchain that runs automatically when its conditions are met, letting people trade, lend or issue tokens without a middleman."],["defi-lending","DeFi lending","DeFi lending is borrowing and lending crypto through smart contracts, where lenders earn interest from a shared pool and borrowers must lock more collateral than they borrow."],["index-price","Index price","The index price is a reference price for an asset calculated from a weighted average of spot prices on several major exchanges, used to anchor futures pricing."],["flash-loan","Flash loan","A flash loan is an uncollateralized DeFi loan that must be borrowed and repaid, with a fee, within the same blockchain transaction, or the whole transaction is cancelled."]],"lesson":null,"coin":{"slug":"chainlink","name":"Chainlink","symbol":"LINK"},"tool":null,"cta":false},"launchpad":{"slug":"launchpad","cat":"exchange-features","en":"Launchpad (token sale)","contentLocale":"en","term":"Launchpad (token sale)","aka":["launchpad","IEO","initial exchange offering"],"short":"A launchpad is a platform, often run by an exchange, where users can buy a new project's tokens in a sale before or as the tokens list for trading.","body":[["Exchange launchpads, a form of IEO or initial exchange offering, select projects and sell a fixed amount of tokens at a set price. Many require you to hold or commit the exchange's own token or a ",["stablecoin","stablecoin"]," during a snapshot or subscription period. If the sale is oversubscribed, each participant receives a share and the unused funds are returned."],["On-chain launchpads exist too, where you connect a wallet and buy tokens through a ",["smart contract","smart-contract"],". These can have fewer checks on projects, so research matters more."],["Key points to check: the sale price and the ",["fully diluted valuation","fully-diluted-valuation"],", how much of the supply is sold, the vesting schedule for team and investor tokens, and when tokens unlock for buyers. Launchpad tokens can fall below the sale price after listing, especially when large unlocks follow. Participation usually needs ",["KYC","kyc"]," and is blocked in some countries."]],"example":["Suppose a launchpad sells 10,000,000 tokens at 0.05 ",["USDT","usdt"],", raising 500,000 USDT, but users commit 25,000,000 USDT. The sale is 50 times oversubscribed. You commit 5,000 USDT, so your allocation is 5,000 divided by 50, which is 100 USDT, buying 2,000 tokens. The other 4,900 USDT is returned. If the token lists at 0.08 USDT, your 2,000 tokens are worth 160 USDT; at 0.03 USDT, they are worth 60 USDT."],"mistakes":["Assuming every launchpad token will trade above its sale price.","Ignoring the vesting and unlock schedule that can add selling pressure after listing.","Joining an unknown on-chain launchpad promoted through private messages."],"faq":[{"q":"What is an IEO in crypto?","a":"An IEO, or initial exchange offering, is a token sale run by an exchange on behalf of a project. The exchange handles the sale and usually lists the token afterwards."},{"q":"Why did I receive only part of my launchpad commitment?","a":"Most sales are oversubscribed, so allocations are cut pro rata. The unused amount is returned to your account after the sale."}],"title":"What is a crypto launchpad? Token sales and IEOs","description":"How crypto launchpads and IEOs work, how oversubscribed allocations are calculated and what to check before joining a token sale.","related":[["launchpool","Launchpool","A Launchpool is an exchange program where you lock tokens you already hold, such as the exchange token or a stablecoin, for a set period to earn a new token before or around its listing."],["token-listing","Token listing","A token listing is when an exchange adds a coin to its markets, letting users deposit, trade and withdraw it on that platform."],["token-unlock","Token unlock","A token unlock is a scheduled event when previously locked tokens, usually held by the team, investors or a treasury, become free to transfer and sell, increasing circulating supply."],["airdrop","Airdrop","An airdrop is a distribution of free tokens to wallet addresses or exchange users, usually to reward early users, decentralize ownership or promote a new project."]],"lesson":null,"coin":null,"tool":"/activities","cta":false},"sub-account":{"slug":"sub-account","cat":"exchange-features","en":"Sub-account","contentLocale":"en","term":"Sub-account","aka":["subaccount","sub account"],"short":"A sub-account is a separate account created under your main exchange account, with its own balances and positions, used to separate strategies, bots or team members.","body":[["Most large exchanges let a verified main account create sub-accounts. Each has its own funds, orders and margin, so a ",["liquidation","liquidation"]," in one does not touch the others. You can move funds between the main account and sub-accounts internally, usually without fees."],"Common uses include keeping a long-term spot portfolio apart from futures trading, running different bots or API keys in separate accounts, giving a team member or fund manager limited access, and tracking performance per strategy. Some sub-accounts can be set to have no withdrawal rights, so the main account controls all transfers out.",["Limits differ by exchange: the number of sub-accounts allowed, whether they share the main account's ",["fee tier","vip-tier"],", and which products each type can access. Sub-accounts normally rely on the main account's ",["KYC","kyc"],". Protect the main account with strong ",["two-factor authentication","two-factor-authentication"],", since it controls everything below it."]],"example":["Suppose you have 12,000 ",["USDT","usdt"],". You keep 8,000 in the main account for spot holdings, move 3,000 into a futures sub-account and 1,000 into a ",["grid bot","grid-trading-bot"]," sub-account. A bad leveraged trade wipes out the 3,000 USDT in the futures sub-account. Your main account still holds 8,000 and the bot sub-account 1,000, because the futures loss could not reach them. Without separation, a cross margin position could have drawn on all 12,000."],"mistakes":["Giving withdrawal permission to API keys used by bots in a sub-account.","Assuming sub-accounts need separate KYC or a separate login method.","Forgetting balances spread across sub-accounts when checking total exposure."],"faq":[{"q":"Why use a sub-account on a crypto exchange?","a":"To separate strategies, limit risk, run bots with dedicated API keys, or let others trade without access to your main funds."},{"q":"Do sub-accounts share the same trading fee level?","a":"On many exchanges they share the main account's fee tier and volume, but rules differ, so check the fee page of your exchange."}],"title":"What is a sub-account on a crypto exchange?","description":"What an exchange sub-account is, how it separates risk and strategies, and how to set permissions for bots and team members safely.","related":[["unified-trading-account","Unified trading account (UTA)","A unified trading account (UTA) is an exchange account mode that lets you trade spot, margin, futures and sometimes options from one balance, with assets shared as collateral."],["isolated-margin","Isolated margin","Isolated margin is a margin mode where each position has its own separate collateral, so a liquidation can only take the margin assigned to that position."],["two-factor-authentication","Two-factor authentication (2FA)","Two-factor authentication (2FA) is a login and approval method that asks for a second proof, such as a code from an authenticator app or a security key, in addition to your password."],["copy-trading","Copy trading","Copy trading is an exchange feature that automatically mirrors another trader's positions in your own account, in proportion to the amount you allocate."]],"lesson":null,"coin":null,"tool":null,"cta":false},"vip-tier":{"slug":"vip-tier","cat":"exchange-features","en":"VIP fee tier","contentLocale":"en","term":"VIP fee tier","aka":["VIP level","fee tier","VIP program"],"short":"A VIP fee tier is an exchange level that gives lower trading fees and extra benefits to users who trade high volume or hold large balances.","body":[["Exchanges publish a fee schedule with several levels. The base level applies to most users. Higher levels, often called VIP 1, VIP 2 and so on, cut ",["maker and taker fees","maker-taker-fees"]," step by step. Qualification usually depends on 30-day ",["trading volume","trading-volume"],", total asset balance or holdings of the exchange's token, and is often re-checked daily."],["Spot and futures volumes may be counted separately, with separate tiers. Top levels can reach zero or even negative maker fees, which means the exchange pays you for adding ",["liquidity","liquidity"],". VIP status can also bring higher withdrawal limits, a dedicated account manager or better borrowing rates."],"For most retail traders, the base tier applies. Some exchanges run VIP trial programs or match a VIP level you hold on another exchange. Check the fee page of your exchange for exact thresholds, since they change and differ widely."],"example":["Suppose the base futures fees are 0.02% maker and 0.05% taker, and VIP 1 is 0.016% maker and 0.04% taker for 30-day volume above 10 million ",["USDT","usdt"],". You trade 12 million USDT a month, all as taker. At the base level you would pay 6,000 USDT. At VIP 1 you pay 4,800 USDT, saving 1,200 USDT. The numbers are illustrative; actual thresholds and rates differ by exchange."],"mistakes":["Overtrading just to reach a VIP tier, increasing risk more than the fee saving is worth.","Forgetting that tier levels are recalculated and can drop when volume falls.","Assuming spot volume counts toward futures VIP levels on every exchange."],"faq":[{"q":"How do you get VIP status on a crypto exchange?","a":"Usually by reaching a 30-day trading volume or asset balance threshold. Some exchanges also offer trial programs or match VIP levels from other exchanges."},{"q":"What is a negative maker fee?","a":"It means the exchange pays you a small rebate for orders that add liquidity to the order book. It is usually only available at high VIP or market maker levels."}],"title":"What is a VIP fee tier on crypto exchanges?","description":"How exchange VIP fee tiers work, how 30-day volume and balances qualify you, and how much lower maker and taker fees can save.","related":[["maker-taker-fees","Maker and taker fees","Maker and taker fees are the two trading fee rates an exchange charges: a maker fee when your order adds liquidity to the order book, and a usually higher taker fee when your order removes it."],["referral-code","Referral code (fee rebate)","A referral code is a code you enter when signing up to an exchange that links your account to a referrer and can give you a discount or rebate on trading fees."],["trading-volume","Trading volume","Trading volume is the total amount of an asset traded over a set period, usually shown as the 24-hour volume in coins or in USD value."],["market-maker","Market maker","A market maker is a trader or firm that continuously places both buy and sell orders on a market, providing liquidity and earning from the spread between them."]],"lesson":null,"coin":null,"tool":"/rates","cta":false},"token-listing":{"slug":"token-listing","cat":"exchange-features","en":"Token listing","contentLocale":"en","term":"Token listing","aka":["listing","new listing","exchange listing"],"short":"A token listing is when an exchange adds a coin to its markets, letting users deposit, trade and withdraw it on that platform.","body":[["Projects apply to exchanges, which review things such as the team, token distribution, ",["smart contract","smart-contract"]," security, ",["liquidity","liquidity"],", legal status and community demand. Large exchanges publish listing announcements with timings for deposits, the opening of trading pairs and withdrawals. Some coins first appear in innovation or seed zones that carry extra risk labels."],["New listings often bring big price swings. Early trading can see sharp spikes and drops as liquidity builds, and some exchanges ",["limit order","limit-order"]," types or price ranges in the first minutes. Tokens can also list on futures markets before or after spot, sometimes as pre-market contracts."],"Watch out for fake listing announcements and fake tokens with the same name. Only trust announcements on the exchange's official channels, and check the contract address before buying a token on-chain. Listing on a major exchange does not mean the exchange endorses the project."],"example":["Suppose an exchange announces that a new token opens for trading at 10:00 UTC. In the first 5 minutes the price jumps from 0.50 to 1.20 ",["USDT","usdt"],", then settles around 0.70 within an hour. A buyer who used a ",["market order","market-order"]," at the peak with 1,200 USDT got about 1,000 tokens, now worth about 700 USDT, a loss of roughly 42%. A buyer who placed a limit order at 0.60 got 2,000 tokens for the same 1,200 USDT, worth about 1,400 USDT at 0.70."],"mistakes":["Placing large market orders in the first minutes of a new listing.","Trusting listing rumours or screenshots from social media instead of official announcements.","Buying a token with the same name on a DEX without checking the contract address."],"faq":[{"q":"Does a token's price go up after an exchange listing?","a":"Not reliably. Prices often swing sharply both ways after listing, and many new tokens fall below their early trading levels."},{"q":"How do exchanges decide which tokens to list?","a":"Each exchange has its own review process, typically looking at the project's team, tokenomics, security, liquidity, legal factors and user demand."}],"title":"What is a token listing? How new coins list on exchanges","description":"What a token listing is, how exchanges review and announce new coins, and why prices swing so much in the first trading minutes.","related":[["delisting","Delisting","Delisting is when an exchange removes a token or trading pair, ending trading and usually setting a deadline to withdraw the remaining balances."],["launchpool","Launchpool","A Launchpool is an exchange program where you lock tokens you already hold, such as the exchange token or a stablecoin, for a set period to earn a new token before or around its listing."],["liquidity","Liquidity","Liquidity is how easily you can buy or sell an asset quickly, in size, without moving its price much."],["altcoin","Altcoin","An altcoin is any cryptocurrency other than Bitcoin, a broad category that includes Ether, Solana, stablecoins, exchange tokens and memecoins."]],"lesson":null,"coin":null,"tool":"/activities","cta":false},"stock-perpetuals":{"slug":"stock-perpetuals","cat":"tokenized-assets","en":"Stock perpetuals","contentLocale":"en","term":"Stock perpetuals","aka":["stock perps","equity perpetuals","stock perpetual futures"],"short":"Stock perpetuals are perpetual futures contracts that track the price of a share or stock index, letting you go long or short with leverage without owning the stock.","body":[["They work like crypto ",["perpetual futures","perpetual-futures"],". You post margin, usually in ",["USDT","usdt"]," or ",["USDC","usdc"],", and open a long or short position. There is no expiry date. A ",["funding rate","funding-rate"]," paid between longs and shorts keeps the ",["contract","smart-contract"]," close to the ",["index price","index-price"],", which is based on the underlying stock's price."],"Because stock markets close at night and on weekends, venues handle off-hours differently. Some keep trading using internal pricing, some widen limits, and some pause or switch to a different price source. Gaps when the stock reopens can move the mark price sharply and trigger liquidations.","Stock perpetuals give price exposure only. You own no shares, receive no dividends in the normal way and have no shareholder rights. Availability depends on the platform and your country. Check the contract specification page for funding interval, trading hours, leverage limits and how the index is calculated."],"example":"Suppose a stock perpetual tracks a share at 100 USD and you go long 10 contracts of 1 share each with 5x leverage, posting 200 USD margin for a 1,000 USD position. On Monday the stock opens 12% lower at 88 USD after weekend news. Your position loses 120 USD, 60% of your margin. With a 20% drop, the loss would be 200 USD, your full margin, and you would be liquidated before that point.","mistakes":["Holding leveraged positions over a weekend without planning for opening gaps.","Expecting dividends or shareholder rights from a perpetual contract.","Ignoring funding payments, which can add up on longer holds."],"faq":[{"q":"What is the difference between stock perpetuals and tokenized stocks?","a":"Tokenized stocks are tokens backed by real shares. Stock perpetuals are derivatives that track the price, use margin and leverage, and do not involve owning shares."},{"q":"Do stock perpetuals trade on weekends?","a":"It depends on the platform. Some trade around the clock with special pricing rules, others restrict trading when the stock market is closed."}],"title":"What are stock perpetuals? Stock perps explained","description":"How stock perpetual futures work, how funding and off-hours pricing apply, and how they differ from owning tokenized stocks.","related":[["tokenized-stocks","Tokenized stocks","Tokenized stocks are blockchain tokens designed to track the price of a company's shares, usually backed one to one by real shares held by a custodian."],["perpetual-futures","Perpetual futures (perps)","Perpetual futures (perps) are futures contracts with no expiry date that use a periodic funding rate to keep their price close to the underlying spot price."],["funding-rate","Funding rate","A funding rate is a periodic payment between long and short traders that keeps a perpetual futures price close to the spot price."],["leverage","Leverage","Leverage is using borrowed funds or margin to open a position larger than your own capital, which multiplies both profits and losses."]],"lesson":null,"coin":null,"tool":"/topics/tokenized-stocks-vs-perpetuals","cta":true}});
